analytical.level.body></analytical.level><analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0"><front date.updated="0"><outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><designator date.updated="0">B</designator><name date.updated="0">Legislative and Regulatory Framework</name></outline.name.block></front><analytical.level.body date.updated="0"><section.block><section date.revised="0" date.updated="0"><metadata.block metadata.store="yes"><metadata field="classification"><value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value></metadata></metadata.block><section.front date.updated="0"><outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><label date.updated="0">&sect;</label><designator date.updated="0">8:7</designator><name date.updated="0">Introduction</name></outline.name.block></section.front><section.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Manitoba has been active in the development of legislation dealing with protection of the environment and human health and safety within the confines of Manitoba&apos;s constitutional powers. This section will describe the effects of existing Manitoba legislation that is not specific to solely addressing climate change issues on GHG regulation and the province&apos;s response to climate change.</para.text></para></section.body></section><section date.revised="0" date.updated="0"><metadata.block metadata.store="yes"><metadata field="classification"><value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value></metadata></metadata.block><section.front date.updated="0"><outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><label date.updated="0">&sect;</label><designator date.updated="0">8:8</designator><name date.updated="0">Environment<sep/>General Legislation</name></outline.name.block></section.front><section.body date.updated="0"><head align="l" toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><name date.updated="0"><ital><bold>The Environment Act</bold></ital><footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><ital>The Environment Act</ital>, C.C.S.M., c. E125.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></name></name.block></head><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">The express purpose of <ital>The Environment Act</ital> is to develop and maintain an environmental management system in Manitoba that will sustain a high quality of life, including social and economic development, recreation and leisure for current and future generations of Manitobans. It is intended to complement and support the provincial government&apos;s planning and policy mechanisms. It is the legislation under which the environmental assessment of projects that have significant effects on the environment are conducted (as discussed below). It also provides mechanisms for public consultation in environmental decision making.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><ital>The Environment Act</ital>, C.C.S.M., c. E125, s. 1.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> With respect to proposals for Class 3 developments, the Act requires &ldquo;the amount of greenhouse gases to be generated by the proposed development and the energy efficiency of the proposed development&rdquo; to be taken into account in addition to other potential environmental impacts.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><ital>The Environment Act</ital>, C.C.S.M., c. E125, s. 12.0.2.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><trace>At the time the regulation was introduced, relatively few regulations under the Environment Act were directed toward climate change mitigation, like for example, the Coal and Petroleum Coke Ban for Space Heating Regulation. While Manitoba has since adopted climate related legislation, the majority of the rules under the Act continue to concern environmental protection more broadly.</trace><trace.deleted/> However, some of the regulations may nonetheless have incidental effects on total GHG emissions in that they restrict certain activities or require licensing of certain developers and developments. Licensing decisions may include conditions related to GHG emissions. These regulations include: <ital>The Burning of Crop Residue and Non-Crop Herbage Regulation</ital>;<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><ital>The Burning of Crop Residue and Non-Crop Herbage Regulation</ital>, Man. Reg. 77/93 (as amended).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote><ital>The Disposal of Whey Regulation</ital>;<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><ital>The Disposal of Whey Regulation</ital>, Man. Reg. 90/88R (as amended).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote><ital>The Incinerators Regulation</ital>;<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><ital>The Incinerators Regulation</ital>, Man. Reg. 91/88R (as amended).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote><ital>The Inco Limited and Hudson Bay Mining and Smelting Co., Limited Smelter Complex Regulation</ital>;<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><ital>The Inco Limited and Hudson Bay Mining and Smelting Co., Limited Smelter Complex Regulation</ital>, Man. Reg. 165/88, which deals with smelter emissions from two of Manitoba&apos;s largest GHG and other pollutant emitters located in Flin Flon and Thompson, Manitoba.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote><ital>The Livestock Manure and Mortalities Management Regulation</ital>;<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">9</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><ital>The Livestock Manure and Mortalities Management Regulation</ital>, Man. Reg. 42/98 (as amended).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote><ital>The Peat Smoke Control Regulation</ital>;<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">10</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><ital>The Peat Smoke Control Regulation</ital>, Man. Reg. 226/89 (as amended).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> and <ital>The Waste Management FacilitiesRegulation</ital>.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">11</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><ital>The Waste Management Facilities Regulation</ital>, Man. Reg. 37/2016 (as amended).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></para.text></para><head align="l" date.trace="1" date.updated="1" toc.include="y"><name.block date.updated="0" toc.include="y"><name date.updated="0"><bold><ital>The Carbon Capture and Storage Act</ital></bold><footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">*</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">John Stefaniuk, &ldquo;Not Just Hot Air: Manitoba Introduces The Captured Carbon Storage Act&rdquo; (11 June 2024), online (article): <cite finder="legacy" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref">https://www.tdslaw.com/resource/not-just-hot-air-manitoba-introduces-the-captured-carbon-storage-act/</ref></cite></para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></name></name.block></head><footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">9</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">John Stefaniuk, &ldquo;Not Just Hot Air: Manitoba Introduces The Captured Carbon Storage Act&rdquo; (11 June 2024), online (article): <cite finder="legacy" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref">https://www.tdslaw.com/resource/not-just-hot-air-manitoba-introduces-the-captured-carbon-storage-act/</ref></cite></para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote><para date.updated="1" date.trace="1"><para.text date.updated="0">Manitoba Bill 31, known as <ital>the Carbon Capture and Storage Act</ital> (CCS Act), represents a significant legislative effort to combat climate change through the regulation and promotion of carbon capture and storage (CCS) technologies. Introduced in the Manitoba Legislature, this bill aims to provide a comprehensive legal framework for the development, implementation, and management of CCS projects within the province.</para.text></para><para date.updated="1" date.trace="1"><para.text date.updated="0">Bill 31, <ital>The Carbon Capture and Storage Act</ital> (CCS Act) also aligns with federal CO2 emission credits allowing a cohesive strategy for reducing greenhouse gas emissions. The federal government has implemented a carbon pricing system that includes a carbon tax and an output-based pricing system (OBPS) for large industrial emitters. The CCS Act in Manitoba complements these federal efforts by:</para.text>
                                <list list.style="block" date.updated="1" date.trace="1"><list.item date.updated="1" date.trace="1"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><bold>Compliance Reducing Costs:</bold> CCS projects developed under the CCS Act can significantly reduce the carbon emissions of industrial facilities. This reduction helps these facilities lower their carbon tax liabilities or receive credits under the OBPS, which sets a cap on emissions and provides credits for staying below that cap.</para.text></para></list.item><list.item date.updated="1" date.trace="1"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><bold>Generating Emission Credits</bold>: By capturing and storing CO2, facilities can generate emission reduction credits. These credits can be traded or used to meet federal compliance obligations, integrating seamlessly with the federal carbon pricing system.</para.text></para></list.item></list></para><para date.updated="1" date.trace="1"><para.text date.updated="0">The primary objectives of the Carbon Capture and Storage Act are to establish a legal and regulatory framework for CCS activities, ensure the safe, efficient, and environmentally responsible implementation of CCS projects, promote investment in CCS technologies and infrastructure within Manitoba, and align provincial climate policies with national and international greenhouse gas reduction commitments.</para.text></para><para date.updated="1" date.trace="1"><para.text date.updated="0">The enactment of Bill 31 positions Manitoba as a leader in climate change mitigation through innovative technologies. By providing a structured approach to CCS, the province aims to reduce its carbon footprint and contribute to Canada&apos;s national emissions reduction targets, attract investment in clean technology and green infrastructure, potentially boosting the local economy, and set a precedent for other provinces and regions to develop similar legislative frameworks for CCS</para.text></para><head align="l" toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><name date.updated="0"><bold><ital>The Sustainable Development Act</ital> (Repealed)</bold><footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">12</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><ital>The Sustainable Development Act</ital>, C.C.S.M., c. S270 (repealed).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></name></name.block></head><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Manitoba became one of the first jurisdictions in Canada to enact sustainability legislation in the form of <ital>The Sustainable Development Act</ital>, which came into force on June 28, 1997. Its <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;19" nep=";;21" hyphenated="n" folio="8-19" backFolio="8-20" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="4" releaseDate="2022;12;30" ?>purpose was to create a legislative framework through which principles of sustainable development were to be implemented in the provincial public sector and promoted in private industry and society in general.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">13</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><ital>The Sustainable Development Act</ital>, C.C.S.M., c. S270, s. 2 (repealed).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> <ital>The Sustainable Development Act</ital> was repealed in November 2018 by <ital>The 2018 Climate and Green Plan Act</ital>.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">14</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><ital>The Climate and Green Plan Act</ital>, C.C.S.M., c. C134, s.13(b).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">The Department of Sustainable Development then acted under the authority of several other provincial statutes<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">15</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">These statutes include but are not limited to: <ital>The Biofuels Act</ital>, C.C.S.M. c. B40; <ital>The Dangerous Goods Handling and Transportation Act</ital>, C.C.S.M. c. D12; <ital>The Environment Act</ital>, C.C.S.M. c. E125; <ital>The Ozone Depleting Substances Act</ital>, C.C.S.M. c. O80; <ital>The Pimachiowin Aki World Heritage Fund Act</ital>, C.C.S.M. c. P70; <ital>The Water Resources Conservation Act</ital>, C.C.S.M. c. W72; <ital>The Wildfires Act</ital>, C.C.S.M. c. W128; and <ital>The Wildlife Act</ital>, C.C.S.M. c. W130.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> in addition to <ital>The 2018 Climate and Green Plan Act</ital>. The repeal of <ital>The Sustainable Development Act</ital> along with <ital>The 2008 Climate Change Act</ital> (also repealed by <ital>2018 Climate and Green Plan Act</ital>) led to a reduction of over 20 regulatory requirements.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">16</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bb70e205f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba Sustainable Development, Annual Report 2018-2019 (2019) at p. 202</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb70e215f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.gov.mb.ca/sd/pubs/annual-reports/annual_report_2018_19.pdf</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> The department, now the Department of Environment, Climate and Parks, works to incorporate previous principles and guidelines under the former <ital>Sustainable Development Act</ital> (repealed) into department activities, programs and business practices<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">17</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bb735305f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba Sustainable Development, Annual Report 2018-2019 (2019) at p. 153</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb735315f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.gov.mb.ca/sd/pubs/annual-reports/annual_report_2018_19.pdf</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022). See also Manitoba Round Table on Environment and Economy, Principles and Guidelines of Sustainable Development, at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb75c405f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.gov.mb.ca/mr/plups/pdf/pgsd.pdf</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> and continues to be the department responsible for providing services to the government for programs related to sustainable development and now also climate change.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">18</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bb75c415f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba Sustainable Development, Annual Report 2018-2019 (2019) at p. 18</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb75c425f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.gov.mb.ca/sd/pubs/annual-reports/annual_report_2018_19.pdf</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></para.text></para><head align="l" toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><name date.updated="0"><bold>The Water Resources Conservation Act</bold><footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">19</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><ital>The Water Resources Conservation Act</ital>, C.C.S.M., c. W72.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></name></name.block></head><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><ital>The Water Resources Conservation Act</ital> is not directed specifically to GHG reduction but it includes direct reference to climate change in the context of uncertainty related to climate change impacts.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">20</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Its preamble provides: &ldquo;AND WHEREAS, in light of the fact that future domestic needs and the potential effects of climate change are unknown, such a scheme should be based on the precautionary principle and on sustainable water resource management practices&rdquo;.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> The purpose of the Act is to define water basins and sub-basins in Manitoba, to prohibit the inter-basin transfer of water without prior authorization and to provide authority for the enactment of regulations related to the subject matter of the Act.</para.text></para></section.body></section><section date.revised="0" date.updated="0"><metadata.block metadata.store="yes"><metadata field="classification"><value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value></metadata></metadata.block><section.front date.updated="0"><outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><label date.updated="0">&sect;</label><designator date.updated="0">8:9</designator><name date.updated="0">Environment<sep/>Environmental Assessment Legislation</name></outline.name.block></section.front><section.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><ital>The Environment Act</ital> sets out the requirements and procedure for the environmental approval of proposed developments. Only those developments that fall within the categories specified under the <ital>Classes of Development Regulation</ital><footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><ital>Classes of Development Regulation</ital>, Man. Reg. 164/88 (as amended).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> require licensing. Developments are classified by type based on anticipated environmental impacts and the licensing procedure will vary depending upon the classification. Certain developments may be referred by the Director of Environmental Approvals at Manitoba&apos;s Department of Environment, Climate and Parks, the responsible authority for <ital>The Environment Act</ital> licensing, to the Manitoba Clean Environment Commission for a public hearing.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">See <ital>The Environment Act</ital>, C.C.S.M., c, E125, ss. 10-17.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> For example, large-scale projects must be referred to the Commission. Where projects have been referred to the Commission for review, the Commission generally holds public hearings for the purposes of considering the development. The Commission is required to issue a report that includes recommendations to the Minister as to whether to approve licensing of the development and upon what conditions. The Minister is not obligated to accept the recommendations of the Commission, but is required to provide reasons for refusing to do so.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">See <ital>The Environment Act</ital>, C.C.S.M., c, E125, ss. 10(1), 11(13) and 12(8).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">An amendment to <ital>The Environment Act</ital>, enacted on June 11, 2009 and proclaimed into force December 1, 2009, requires the director or the Minister to take into account&mdash;in addition to other potential environmental impacts of the proposed development&mdash;the amount of greenhouse gases to be generated by the <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;21" nep=";;23" hyphenated="n" folio="8-21" backFolio="8-22" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="4" releaseDate="2022;12;30" ?>proposed development and the energy efficiency of the proposed development.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><ital>The Environment Amendment Act</ital>, S.M. 2009, c. 25, s. 8. See also <ital>The Environment Act</ital>, C.C.S.M., c, E125, s. 12.0.2.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> Although the <ital>Licensing Procedures Regulation</ital> does not contain any specific requirements for proponents to address issues related to climate change, it does require proponents to include information regarding the potential impacts of the development on the environment, including those that relate to the release of pollutants, which can include GHGs.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><ital>Licensing Procedures Regulation</ital>, Man. Reg. 163/88 (as amended).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> Further, even prior to the recent amendments, where matters have been referred by the Minister to the Commission for review and recommendations, the Minister typically included reference to the <ital>Guidelines and Principles for Sustainable Development</ital> in the Commission terms of reference.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">For example, see <cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bb7d1705f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba Clean Environment Commission, &ldquo;Summary of Public Hearings Wuskwatim Generation and Transmission Projects&rdquo; (September 2004), Recommendation 7.9 at p. 121</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb7f8805f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://perma.cc/Y2SN-U2PX</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> This led proponents to comment upon and give evidence in relation to&mdash;and the Commission to inquire as to&mdash;the climate change impacts of the development in the course of the environmental approval process.</para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Manitoba is a party to the <ital>Canada-Manitoba Agreement on Environmental Assessment Cooperation</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><ital>Canada-Manitoba Agreement on Environmental Assessment Cooperation, 2007</ital>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="